              "textField": "The very first lipid lowering medications that were available were the fibrates but they have been largely superseded by the *statins*. The fibrates were named because all of the members of that drug class end in the …*fibrate* suffix. An example of a fibrate that is available in Australia is *fenofibrate*. Fenofibrate increases *HDL*, the good protein but may also increase levels of *LDL*, the bad protein. :(  Nonetheless there are individuals who could benefit from the medication, especially those who have low levels of HDL, the good protein and in *diabetics* who are at risk of losing their limbs, because fenofibrate decreases risk. Fenofibrate should never be taken along with the statins or vitamin B3, which is *nicotinic acid* because of the increased risk of developing *rhabdomyolysis* which is a rapid breakdown of skeletal muscle. The first signs of rhabdomyolysis include muscle pain and *tea coloured urine*."
